learning_objectives:
Component Physical Chemistry:
Upon completion of the course, the student:
- knows the properties of an ideal solution and is able to formulate deviations of ideal behaviour;
- knows the thermodynamic behaviour of electrolyte solutions and can apply the Debye-Hueckel theory;
- can apply the Nernst equation and is able to differentiate between different kinds of electrochemical cells;
- understands the molecular basis of transport, diffusion and migration;
- is knowledgeable about the elementary aspects of the kinetics of electrode reactions;
- is able to apply simple mathematical models of chemical reactions at electrochemical and catalytic surfaces, in combination
with mass transport.
Component Kinetics
Upon completion of the course, the student:
- is able to correctly assimilate, with MS Excel, experimental data in order to determine kinetic parameters, such as the order of
a reaction, reaction rate constants and activation barriers;
- is able to correctly interpret crucial aspects of reaction mechanisms on the basis of kinetic parameters;
- can produce a simple mathematical model of a reaction mechanism with Maple, calculate a solution and display this
graphically;
- can describe, in a semi-quantitative way, the build up of, and the movement on, a potential surface.

Upon completion of the course, the student:
- is knowledgeable about a number of catalytic processes, both homogeneous, heterogeneous, bio-catalytic and electrocatalytic;
- has obtained insight in the most important similarities and dissimilarities, advantages and disadvantages and range of
application of the four different areas of application of catalysis;
- is skilled in the application of the principles of chemical reaction kinetics to catalytic processes and mechanisms;
- has a clear understanding of the mode of action, the molecular mechanism, the preparation, the characterisation and the stability
of a number of catalysts and catalytic processes in the four areas of catalysis;
- knows about the possibilities to immobilize a catalyst;
- is well aware of the important applications of catalysis in industry.

description:
The numerical techniques course focuses on the use of numerical techniques for solving complex issues using a computer. The
course has a focus on the use of such numerical techniques for solving problems in chemistry and chemical engineering. The
following topics are dealt with:
1. Introduction Python; Introduction programming and algorithms;
2. Statistical analysis and plotting of data;
3. Curve fitting and regression;
4. Numerical integration and differentiation;
5. Solving (systems of) linear and nonlinear equations;
6. Numerically solving systems of differential equations;
7. Monte Carlo Methods.

learning_objectives:
Upon completion of the course, the student:
- is familiar with the general concepts of organic chemistry, such as: nucleophile, electrophile, hard and soft, leaving groups,
pKa, intramolecular, cyclo-addition;
- possesses a working knowledge of the reactivity of a broad range of organic compounds;
- has a clear understanding of the relative reactivity of functional groups;
- understands the underlying mechanisms of organic reactions;
- is able to perceive relations with other chemical sub-disciplines.

After following Theoretical Chemistry, the student can:
1.(cid:9)apply the basic algebra that forms the basis for quantum mechanics, i.e., commutator algebra and simple linear algebra;
2.(cid:9)reproduce the postulates of quantum mechanics;
3.(cid:9)write down the equations (possibly to a proportionality constant) that describe how the energy of simple model systems
depends on the quantum numbers that describe these systems;
4.(cid:9)explain, on the basis of simple model systems, the origin of specific quantum phenomena, such as zero-point energy and
tunnelling;
5.(cid:9)describe what fermions and electron spin are, as well as the Paui principle;
6.(cid:9)translate the Pauli principle into the mathematical form of the wave function of many-electron systems;
7.(cid:9)use simple mathematical techniques, such as separation of variables and application of orthogonality conditions, to derive
relevant equations;
8.(cid:9)derive and explain in detail trends that are visible in the periodic table that are based on the Aufbau principle;
9.(cid:9)explain, based on the orbital approximation, what the Hartree method is and what the Hartree-Fock method is, and how those
methods differ;
10.(cid:9)use the secular determinant and the secular equations to determine the energies of small molecules and the associated
eigenfunctions;
11.(cid:9)use simple MO theory to determine the properties of diatomic molecules (for example, whether A2 molecules are
paramagnetic or not) and of triatomic molecules (for example, the shape of AH2, where A = Li-F);
12.(cid:9)explain what the LCAO method is;
13.(cid:9)explain how the Born-Oppenheimer approach can be used for theoretical descriptions of molecular spectroscopy and the
dynamics of chemical reactions.

