At the end of the course the student will be able to:
1.(cid:9)apply the geodesign framework to develop eco-innovative solutions (EIS), which support the spatial transition towards
circularity;
2.(cid:9)formulate the idea of an eco-innovative solution in a way that it responds to challenges formulated by policymakers;
3.(cid:9)represent the - for the transition towards more circularity - most relevant environmental, social and economic subsystems and
their spatial structures;
4.(cid:9)describe, the economic, social and spatial process that are influenced by an EIS, using flow maps, system diagrams and
systemic sections;
5.(cid:9)build a simplified sustainability assessment framework that consists of three indicators;
6.(cid:9)design an alternative future, anticipating the changes in physical spatial structure and resource flows based on the consequence
of the application of an EIS;
7.(cid:9)assess the impact of your EIS using your sustainability framework;
8.(cid:9)to inform decision-makers about the positive and negative effects of your EIS and how they relate to their aims.

