description:
1. Writing in science and engineering: the scientist as a professional writer;
2. Making a story sticky;
3. Story structures:
(cid:9)a. Opening, Challenge, Action, Resolution (OCAR)
(cid:9)b. Action, Background, Development, Climax, Ending (ABDC)
(cid:9)c. Lead, Development (LD)
(cid:9)d. Lead, Development, Resolution (LDR);
4. Targeting different audiences;
5. Internal structure: arcs of tension;
6. Good/bad paragraphs, sentences and words;
7. Flow: connecting the parts;
8. Different levels of reasoning: descriptive, relational, linear, multicomponent;
9. Data presentation, deceptive conclusions and overselling.

learning_objectives:
1. Students are familiar with basic concepts of the structure of a story for different types of writing;
2. Student are familiar with the internal structure of paragraphs and sections;
3. Students are familiar with different types of reasoning;
4. Student can recognize and comment on the story structure (or absence of good structure) in a written text;
5. Students can design a story structure for a text targeted at a specific audience;
6. Student can recognize levels of reasoning and improve the level of reasoning in their own writing;
7. Students are familiar with the optimal way of presenting data and avoiding deceptive conclusions and overselling.

