learning_objectives:
After completing this course, the student can:
Knowledge:
1) explain with your own words basic concepts of soft materials;
2) explain how different experimental techniques can be applied to determine the structure, dynamical response and properties of
a soft material;
Understanding:
3) identify various kinds of intermolecular interactions which determine the structure and dynamics of soft materials at various
length scales;
4) explain how structure and dynamics of soft materials (e.g. polymers, self-assembled systems) can be used for a given product
or application;
Analysis (critical thinking):
5) interpret quantitatively (characterization) experimental data on soft materials;
6) calculate basic quantities related to structure and dynamics of soft materials;
Design:
7) identify how specific properties of a given soft material determine or influence the process method chosen for a given material
application
8) describe various ways of tuning material properties for desired performance in different applications.

At the end of this course you will be able to:
1.(cid:9)Explain the role of Geodesy in Earth Sciences;
2.(cid:9)Analyze the reference systems used in Geodesy in terms of: (i) definition and (ii) the ways their realizations are obtained;
3.(cid:9)Apply coordinate transformations among the reference systems used in Geodesy;
4.(cid:9)Explain how positions can be obtained from GNSS code and carrier phase measurements;
5.(cid:9)Summarize the procedure how to derive the figure of the Earth from gravity observations;
6.(cid:9)Describe the different forms of interaction of electromagnetic radiation with the Earths surface;
7.(cid:9)Describe the different forms of interaction of electromagnetic radiation with the Earths atmosphere;
8.(cid:9)Compare the remote sensing measurement techniques in terms of underlying physical principles and limitations;
9.(cid:9)Select an appropriate survey platform and, if applicable, orbit design to acquire data from a particular phenomenon or process
on Earth by taking into account requirements regarding target accuracy, spatial coverage, and spatial/temporal resolution.

After successful completion of this course, the student:
- can explain the basic principles of satellite altimetry;
- is able to describe the major oceanographic and geodetic applications of satellite altimetry;
- is able to process tide gauge records and satellite altimetry data;
- is able to analyze past sea-level changes based on tide gauge and satellite altimetry observations;
- can explain how different physical processes contribute to sea-level change at global and regional scales.

After taking this course students should be able to
- apply the error propagation laws;
- describe and apply the following estimation principles: weighted least-squares, best linear unbiased estimation, maximum
likelihood estimation;
- set-up an observation model and estimate a set of unknown parameters from a given set of geo-measurements (e.g., remote
sensing, GNSS, gravimetry, surveying);
- make a quality assessment and interpret the estimation results;
- test for errors in measurement data and the observation model;
- devise a regularization approach for ill-conditioned problems.

The students knowledge and skill in the following activities will be increased relative to the intellectual development level
attained during his or her BSc study:
a. The understanding of the principles of project agreements;
b. The understanding and choice of forms of collaboration;
c. The evaluation of alternative forms of contract;
d. The various types of reimbursement;
e. The selection and choice of contract partners;
f. The understanding of the various components of contracts;
g. Foreign contracts .

On completion of this module, you should be able to:
A-LO1: Acquire conceptual (hydro)geological knowledge on project site;
A-LO2: Integrate (hydro-)geological information and knowledge into ground models and estimate resulting uncertainty for a
variety of geotechnical applications
A-LO3: Formulate, explain and solve the (equations of the) finite element method in the context of numerical modelling of
geotechnical applications;
A-LO4: Design and execute numerical simulations for geotechnical applications;
A-LO5: Critically evaluate the results and the limitations of model solutions for geological and geotechnical problems,
considering data and model uncertainty;
A-LO6: Communicate results of geo-engineering investigation and numerical simulations to stakeholders in terms of geo-risks
and opportunities, in the context of a responsible use of the subsurface for multi-purposes.

On completion of this module, the student will be able to
1. understand and apply scientific knowledge that contributes to solving river system-related problems in an ethically sound
manner;
2. analyse and assess the behaviour of river systems and catchment, and assess and predict the system response to interventions,
while taking into account neighbouring/adjacent systems;
3. (a) analyze the problem, (b) formulate functional requirements and constraints, (c) formulate alternative solutions (including
autonomous development), (d) design human interventions in the river or catchment environment for purposes such as flood
protection, nature development or restoration, navigation, freshwater supply, ecology, port operability, water quality, and
construction in water;
4. account for societal, environmental, and economic aspects, and uncertainties in research and design, at various scales;
5. account for the complexities of multidisciplinary environments and the range of stakeholders: (inter)national river
management authorities, nature organisations, NGOs, dredging companies, navigation stakeholders, other governments.

On completion of the module B3 (B03-01 and B03-02), the student will be able to:
LO1. Analyse logistic and operational processes in ports and waterways systems and discuss the capacity controlling parameters;
LO2. Design port/terminal and waterway infrastructure to pre-defined objectives covering economic, societal and environmental
aspects;
LO3. Design interventions in ports and waterways systems, model interactions ranging from simple to complex, between
physical processes and port and waterways systems, and apply simulations for the evaluation of performance;
LO4. Design hard and soft infrastructure interventions to improve port, terminal, and waterway performance including
quantifying trade-offs and discussing these trade-offs with peers;
LO5. Demonstrate the learning over intervention design and performance evaluation in real-life case studies, and
LO6. Apply data science techniques and concepts for a quantitative analysis of processes in port and waterway systems in
various design exercises.

