description:
Information Systems for construction Management (ISM) are part of the asset and construction management processes supported
by various tools, technologies involving the generation and management of digital representations of engineering assets.
The alternative view within this course is to not only represent physical, and functional characteristics of the engineering asset
(3D) but also represent so-called 'design for ty' information characteristics for the entire asset service life.
In this course the following concepts are addressed:
1. Building information modelling and design for ty's;
2. Geometric 3D computer modelling;
3. Constructability within information systems;
4. Maintainability within information systems;
5. Safety within information systems;
6. Sustainability within information systems;
7. ISM future outlook.
This course has a constructivist character, in which the student is guided and encouraged to develop open design knowledge &
solutions (openings) using the self-chosen Project of Interest (PoI) as a connective vehicle enabling the Open Design Learning
(ODL) response. The PoI is either being constructed or already been constructed (must have passed the initial phase)

learning_objectives:
Students must be able to:
1. understand state-of-the-art information systems concepts, principles and practices for a construction project;
2. examine and relate these abstract information systems for construction concepts;
3. rework and transform the project specific information systems concepts and insights;
4. form an individual judgement on these new insights;
5. create a response that integrates all concepts, (computer) models, new insights and developed results;
6. demonstrate the internalization process of the aforementioned learning and development goals and achievements.

Finishing the course, that students are able to:
- Understand and analyse an innovation case, with innovation theory to identify the successes and failures on its adoption;
- Understand specific market structures of the civil engineering domain, the relations between public and private roles in this
domain, and the multifaceted reality thereof;
- Understand the market processes, specifically the public tendering processes for innovation-oriented projects;
- Analyse the technical aspects of a technology / innovation to identify the robustness and the alternative implementations
thereof;
- Analyse the operational context of the projected implementations of a technology;
- Create the role of your business in the pursuit of implementing the technology / innovation;
- Create an adaptive and embedding business plan (flexible and future oriented) for the case.
- From the perspective of a client or an established market party: be able to identify, stimulate, guide and embrace innovative
innovation concepts.

At the end of this course you should be able to:
LO1: explain the role of public clients and public values in construction;
LO2: analyse the context of procuring complex projects;
LO3: critically reflect on the application of project delivery methods;
LO4: design a procurement strategy for complex projects;
LO5: writing a scientifically based advisory report using provided literature.

By completing the CME Research & Development Project, you are able to:
Familiarize yourself with fundamental research and developments related to the topic;
Delineate a research, development or design problem and formulate a methodology to address the research or development
statement in a well-structured way;
Plan, execute and manage the project effectively within the available time and resources;
Demonstrate your understanding of the scientific, societal and ethical context of the CME R&D Project;
Verify, interpret, and critically reflect on obtained results;
Draw relevant conclusions and give useful recommendations;
Present the CME R&D Project in a well-structured manner;

By completing the CME Master Thesis, you are able to:
Familiarize yourself with fundamental research and developments related to the thesis topic;
Demonstrate the capability to independently determine and apply relevant theory;
Delineate a research, development or design problem and formulate a theoretical and/or experimental methodology to answer
the corresponding research question in a well-structured way;
Plan, execute and manage the thesis process effectively considering the available time and resources;
Produce results that are scientifically and technologically relevant;
Verify, interpret, and reflect on obtained results in a critical manner;
Critically reflect on the thesis project at the level of your peers;
Present the thesis project in a well-structured report, or in a draft scientific paper, written in correct English that includes the
problem statement, methodology, scientific context, verification of applied methods, obtained results and critical reflection;
Present the thesis project in a well-structured oral presentation to a wide audience that next to your peers, may include audience
not directly familiar with the thesis topic;
Defend your work, results and reflections in presence of your peers by placing the thesis project into its scientific context and by
explaining the contribution to the field;

description:
The course provides theoretical and practical background for applying data analytics in the field of cyber security. Cyber data
analytics is a huge field with a great diversity of techniques and applications. The course is centered on a selection of seven such
techniques:
learning from imbalanced data;
behavioral profiling and anomaly detection;
sequential data mining;
data stream mining;
learning from software data;
adversarial machine learning;
and privacy-aware data mining;
Anomaly detection is one of the main topics in cyber security. Specific difficulties that the student will learn to handle are the
huge amounts of data and the large number of false positives. Behavioral profiling applies to both people and software processes.
Different techniques will be taught to construct profiles from software logs. While building such profiles, care should be taken to
not infringe upon the privacy of individuals the data is collected from. Finally, attackers will modify their behavior in order to
avoid being detected, a cyber data analytics engineer tries to make their models/profiles robust against such modifications.

The course provides theoretical and practical background for applying data analytics in the field of cyber security. Cyber data
analytics is a huge field with a great diversity of techniques and applications. The course is centered on a selection of seven such
techniques:
learning from imbalanced data;
behavioral profiling and anomaly detection;
sequential data mining;
data stream mining;
learning from software data;
adversarial machine learning;
and privacy-aware data mining;
