description:
Omics perform the collective characterization and quantification of biological molecules that translate into the structure,
function, and dynamics of organisms.
This course aims to provide fundamentals and practical aspects of the state-of-the-art of omics technologies used to study pure
and mixed microbial cultures such as whole (meta)genome sequencing, (meta)proteomics and metabolomics. The course also
provides examples of how those data can be used to obtain information on the structure and function of microorganisms.
Fundamentals of omics techniques:
(cid:9)Whole-genome and shotgun metagenome sequencing;
(cid:9)Large-scale (meta)proteomics;
(cid:9)(Meta)metabolomics;
(cid:9)Brief introduction into other, more specialized omics approaches.
Omics data handling and interpretation:
(cid:9)Introduction to generic omics processing pipelines (where applicable);
(cid:9)Data handling such as interpretation of omics data (e.g. discussing published datasets).

learning_objectives:
After this course, the students will be able to:
- evaluate the advantages and limitations of various omics techniques;
- interpret data obtained from various omics techniques;
- design appropriate omics experiments to study pure and mixed microbial cultures;
- work in small groups and communicate large scale omics data.

