By the end of the course, the student must be able to
- model real-world control and decision-making problems under uncertainty and in dynamic environments using the framework
of discrete-time Markov decision process;
- compute relevant technical concepts including hitting probabilities, expected hitting times, invariant distributions, and the long-
run proportion of time spent in a given state;
- formalize the optimal decision-making problem and (rigorously) derive the respective dynamic programming principle and
useful properties of the desired solution;
- use the dynamic programming principle and solve the problem using different computational approaches;
- explore the technical literature in control and operations research, and undertake independent self-study and research in the
future.

