description:
Common trade-offs in Logistics and Supply Chain Systems:
1) Fundamentals of logistics and supply chain systems;
2) Methods for analyzing logistics and supply chain systems on multiple levels;
3) Strategic, Tactical and Operational Trade-Offs in logistics and supply chain systems;
4) Game Concepts to facilitate improvement of the awareness of trade-offs in logistics and supply chain systems.
5) Discussing trade-offs and ideas of game concepts.
Game Essentials:
- Sharing (board and computer) game experiences and discussing fundamentals;
- Flow experiences (Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi);
- Game Concept Decompositions and Construction;
- Triadic Game Design approach;
- Design Methods (from established needs to workable results);
- Playing and Learning:
- Lemonade Tycoon;
- Rise of Industry;
- Playing MSc-Students developed games.
Game Design Assignment:
- Towards understanding supply chain problem situations;
- Developing Game Session Objectives;
- Setting Boundaries;
- In Depth Analysis of the problem situation and value of possible solutions;
- Setting Requirements;
- Developing the Game (Session) Concept;
- Generating Solutions;
- Evaluating Solutions;
- Game Delivery and Reporting.

learning_objectives:
After this course students are able to:
(cid:9)evaluate and analyse the performance of energy (electricity) markets;
(cid:9)identify cases of market failures and design remedial action to rectify these market failures;
(cid:9)estimate the LCOE and calculate the NPV and IRR of an electricity investment project;
(cid:9)contrast private and social costs of (renewable) electricity generation;
(cid:9)assess technological lock-in in energy generation; and
(cid:9)appraise the role of public policy in energy markets and in energy technology innovation.

After the Graduation Project the student is able:
- to read and understand international literature of the topic researched;
- to work independently on an academic level in a R&D environment;
- to plan and execute research in a project of 45 ECTS;
- to work in an interdisciplinary and multicultural team of experts and supporting technologists/analysts;
- to present his/her results in English by means of a presentation to a professional audience and by means of a written scientific
report.

You
- are familiar with contextual, cultural and institutional factors that structure science, engineering and policy outcomes;
- are familiar with challenges for the future of science and society;
- and can interpret these challenges and factors from a communication perspective;
- can ask questions and take a critical-constructive attitude when presented with complex real-life problems in the socio-technical
realm;
- are capable of critical self-reflection, related to your potential role in the science communication field;
- can translate science, present your findings and participate in the academic, institutional and/or public debate.

You
... can recognize and describe concepts related to personal professional development and understand the interrelations between
them;
... can apply these concepts in analyzing specific cases, in developing an interview protocol and a tool for personal professional
development;
... can discuss these concepts with professionals;
... can critically reflect on your own personal professional identity
... can sketch your own desired development, detect your personal way to professionalize and identify initiatives to develop
personally and professionally;
... have insight into concrete opportunities to professionalize within your own field;
... can work in collaboration with others to develop a tool for professionalization.

Knowledge:
- You can name and describe various communication (including marketing) approaches relevant to innovation development;
Understanding:
- You can identify and explain the synergies between communication and innovation management;
Application:
- You can identify, structure and translate complex information with regard to the integration of communication and innovation;
- You are able to write, with your team, a written advice about communication aspects of the development of (high-tech)
innovations;
Reflection
- You can discuss, critically evaluate and qualify on communication relevant to innovation development, and put these in a
societal perspective.

You can:
Learning objectives
articulate relevant personal and substantive learning objectives;
articulate challenging learning objectives;
detect a personal way to put the learning objectives into practice;
estimate realistically what results will be feasible within the internship period;
report on your performance and achievements with regard to his learning objectives;
Performance
successfully perform the tasks that you and your company supervisor agreed upon;
perform tasks independently / on an academic level;
have a positive attitude towards work and colleagues;
Reflection
critically reflect on the achievement of the learning objectives (learning outcome);
critically reflect on the process and output of his internship.

description:
Bij vakdidactiek gaat het om een analyse van de lesstof. Enerzijds vanuit het vakgebied en het daarvan afgeleide schoolvak en
anderzijds vanuit leerlingdenkbeelden en voorkennis. Deze analyse wordt gebruikt voor het ontwikkelen van een aantal lessen
waarin vaktaal, voorkennis en de inhoud van het schoolvak belangrijke aspecten zijn.
