Global objectives:
After this course students are able to
-(cid:9)state definition and properties of fundamental objects of real harmonic analysis;
-(cid:9)apply key techniques of harmonic analysis in a different context;
-(cid:9)connect and apply the theory to the theory of partial differential equations.
Objectives per topic:
After this course students are able to
-(cid:9)apply covering lemmas in various settings;
-(cid:9)apply maximal operators in boundedness arguments;
-(cid:9)apply real and complex interpolation;
-(cid:9)state what Hardy and BMO spaces are and where they are used;
-(cid:9)explain the importance of John-Nirenberg type arguments;
-(cid:9)verify if a given operator is a singular integral operator;
-(cid:9)state the most important examples of singular integral operators;
-(cid:9)show the L^p boundedness of singular integral operators;
-(cid:9)give a proof of Mikhlins multiplier theorem;
-(cid:9)apply Mikhlins multiplier theorem to elliptic PDEs;
-(cid:9)explain the basics of Littlewood-Paley theory.

After the first part of the course the student can prove existence, (short-time) uniqueness, and regularity of solutions to equations
from fluid dynamics, namely
(cid:9)* the time-independent Stokes equations
(cid:9)* the time-dependent Stokes equations
(cid:9)* the Navier-Stokes equations.
After the second part of the course the student can
(cid:9)* formulate some nonlinear dispersive equations as Hamiltonian systems;
(cid:9)* derive conservation laws from symmetries of Hamiltonian system;
(cid:9)* characterize traveling waves as critical points of the Hamiltonian functional;
(cid:9)* prove that (constrained) minimizers of this functional are (non)linearly stable;
(cid:9)* analyse when saddle points of this functional are (non)linearly unstable.
(cid:9)* apply the theory to prove (in)stability of solutions of model equations

description:
This course on Monte Carlo simulation and stochastic processes consists of a mixture of these components:
modelling and models (and some of their pluses and minuses),
learning a number of stochastic processes and some of their properties,
doing Monte Carlo simulations,
analyzing the generated data and drawing conclusions from them.
Contents and format of this course are intended to combine the following:
1. real (possibly stylized) problems as starting points;
2. enough about modelling to make participants aware of the impact of modelling decisions;
3. identification of a number of stochastic processes present in/related to models and problems;
4. (limited) treatment of underlying theory, guided by:
(a) what is necessary to ensure that participants know how to do things right and
(b) what enables them to do things smartly;
5. facts, properties, and theorems:
(a) sincere attempt to understand where possible;
(b) focus on practical implications/uses;
(c) illustrations rather than proofs/theory;
6. useful tools and techniques from statistics, probability, and stochastic processes;
7. simulation as aid to: obtaining answers, intuitive understanding, illustration of theory;
8. providing as much opportunity as feasible for participants to work on/with problems from their own field.
Tentative topics/contents list:
Review basic probability: most as self-study, some in class; self-diagnostic test in week 3 or 4 during class hours. Models,
modelling. Simulation: Convert a model to a program and run it. How to expose and locate errors. Data, empirical distribution;
estimation. Accuracy, efficiency. Sensitivity. Rare event simulation. Experimental design. Conditional distributions and
expectations; conditioning as a tool. Multivariate distributions: general (some examples); multivariate normal. Stochastic
processes: Random walk. Poisson process; renewal process. Markov chains: discrete time; continuous time; embedded Markov
chain; birth and death process; Brownian motion. A snippet of diffusions, (solutions to) SDEs.

learning_objectives:
Learning objectives: after following the course, participants will be able to
1. in an iid setting, carry out Monte Carlo simulations; and
2. extract and collect, summarize, and interpret the data generated; and
3. assess accuracy/uncertainty in estimates, and solve questions like How many runs for 1% accuracy?;
4. the above (three) also in some non-iid settings, e.g., for a stationary process, using the batch-means method;
5. for small-sized practical problems: build a suitable model and simulate it;
6. extract and collect required model parameters from a problem description or background;
7. recognize where modelling choices are made;
8. make elementary modelling decisions themselves;
9. recognize and describe in mathematical terms (the properties of) a number of stochastic processes, among others: Markov
chains, random walks, Brownian motion;
10. recognize/identify the covered stochastic processes in practical settings/problems;
11. use the tools and concepts from probability theory to describe, discuss, and manipulate (the components of) stochastic
models;
12. where applicable, solve problems by means other than Monte Carlo, i.e., analytically or numerically, procuring exact
answers, by identifying a stochastic process and using its known properties; for example, concerning long-term system-
behaviour.

