At the end of the course the student will
1. have a good overview of the current state-of-the-art in quantum algorithms and their applications;
2. be able to understand the recent literature on quantum algorithms;
3. be able to analyse the theoretical properties of quantum algorithms;
4. be able to implement quantum algorithms for practical applications;
5. be able to run quantum-assisted applications on quantum simulators and real NISQ devices;
6. be able to perform independent research on this topics, e.g. in a master project.

The course aims to introduce the main features of dynamical systems as models in applied mathematics described in terms of
differential equations.
At the end of this course the student will be able to:
- apply the center manifold theory to analyse stability of a dynamical system;
- simplify dynamical systems using the normal forms' method;
- analyse local and global bifurcations;
- prove that the dynamical system is chaotic;
- apply techniques of fractional calculus.

1.(cid:9)The student is able to design a research project:
-(cid:9)The student is able to use, explain and justify adequate research and design methodologies;
-(cid:9)The student is able to apply theory to the performed project;
-(cid:9)The student is able to use techniques for interpretation and verification and bases his/her conclusions on results;
-(cid:9)The student is able to do reliable work with scientific significance.
2.(cid:9)The student is able to execute a research project:
-(cid:9)The student has a critical attitude towards his/her own results, literature and specialists;
-(cid:9)The student makes an original contribution to the project;
-(cid:9)The student takes initiative (together with the supervisor) to give his/her own input within the research project;
-(cid:9)The student interacts sufficiently with peers and superiors;
-(cid:9)The student is able to make and execute a project plan.
3.(cid:9)The student is able to write a research report:
-(cid:9)The student is able to write a research report that shows sufficient coherence of content;
-(cid:9)The students is able to structure the research report and sufficiently present the content (text and figures);
-(cid:9)The student expresses argumentation using correct spelling and grammar;
4.(cid:9)The student is able to present and defend the research project:
-(cid:9)The student is able to present the content using sufficient detail to support conclusions;
-(cid:9)The student is able to logical structure the presentation and use visual aids;
-(cid:9)The student is able to adequately formulate and express himself/herself as well as sufficiently address the audience;
-(cid:9)The student is able to argument and answer the questions asked by the committee.

